EXPERIMENT 21 – EQUIVALENCE CLASS PARTITIONING (MANUAL TESTING)
AIM
To create test cases for a simple calculator application using Equivalence Class Partitioning.

Equivalence Class Partitioning divides input data into classes in which all values are expected to behave in the same way.
ALGORITHM
1. List the calculator inputs: first number, second number and operation (+, -, *, /).
2. Divide inputs into valid and invalid equivalence classes.
3. Select one representative value from every class.
4. Prepare test cases with test case ID, class, input and expected result.
5. Number the classes as V1, V2 for valid and I1, I2 for invalid.
6. Ensure every class is covered by at least one test case.
7. Execute test cases, compare actual and expected results, and record status.
INPUT CONDITIONS AND EQUIVALENCE CLASSES
Input condition	Valid classes	Invalid classes
First and second number	V1: Positive integer; V2: Negative integer; V3: Zero	I1: Non-numeric; I2: Blank; I3: Above 9999; I4: Below -9999; I5: Decimal
Operation	V4: +, -, *, /	I6: No operation selected
Divisor	V5: Non-zero number	I7: Zero


